      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the veal in a large saucepan with the onion, carrot, celery, bay leaves, peppercorns and white wine. Cover with cold water. Bring slowly to just below a simmer over medium heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Keep the liquid at a very gentle poach . surface should barely tremble, never boil. Cook for 60 to 75 minutes until the veal reaches an internal temperature of 65 degrees Celsius or a skewer slides through with no resistance."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the veal and let it cool to room temperature. Wrap tightly in cling film and refrigerate for at least 2 hours, or overnight."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the tonnato sauce. Blend the tuna, anchovies, capers and egg yolks in a blender until smooth. With the motor running, drizzle in the olive oil slowly until the sauce is thick and creamy. Add the lemon juice, season to taste and blend again briefly. If the sauce is very thick, thin with a tablespoon of the poaching liquid."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Slice the cold veal as thinly as possible, ideally 2 to 3 mm thick. Arrange on a large flat serving plate."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spoon the tonnato sauce generously over the veal, covering each slice. Scatter over extra capers. Chill for 30 minutes before serving to let the sauce settle into the meat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Veal is dry and crumbly when sliced",
              "cause": "The poaching liquid boiled rather than held at a gentle simmer",
              "fix": "Keep the heat very low next time; the surface of the liquid should barely move throughout cooking"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Tonnato sauce is too thick to spoon",
              "cause": "Too much olive oil was added relative to the other ingredients",
              "fix": "Add poaching liquid a tablespoon at a time and blend briefly until the sauce reaches a pourable consistency"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Sauce tastes very fishy",
              "cause": "The anchovies were too dominant in the blend ratio",
              "fix": "Add a squeeze more lemon juice and a little extra tuna to balance the anchovy flavour"
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Vitello tonnato comes from Piedmont and has been on the tables of the region since at least the 1800s. It is a summer dish, served cold at large lunches and as part of antipasto spreads. The tuna and veal combination sounds strange but works completely. It is one of those dishes where you just have to trust the recipe."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
